---
title: "Form field"
eyebrow: "Components"
lead: "A 56px box with the label inside it. The only border is a rule along the bottom."
permalink: /components/form-field/
---
{% from "components.njk" import field %}
<section aria-labelledby="basic-h">
  <h2 id="basic-h" class="type-h2">Single line and message</h2>
  <p>The label sits inside the field and floats up when the field has focus or text. It is a real <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> connected to a real <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>, so browsers and screen readers treat it as a normal field. Fields that ask about the person filling in the form, such as name and email, also carry an <code>autocomplete</code> value (<code>name</code>, <code>email</code>), so browsers and password managers can fill them in. Click into these to try it.</p>
  <p>Every field here is required, and the field carries the <code>required</code> attribute, so a screen reader says so. Sighted people get a sentence instead. Put <strong>All fields are required.</strong> above the first field of the form, as in the demo below.</p>
  {% demo "Single-line and multi-line fields, with the required sentence above the first field", "form" %}
  <p>All fields are required.</p>
  {{ field("ff-name", "name", "Name", autocomplete="name") }}
  {{ field("ff-message", "message", "Message", multiline=true) }}
  {% enddemo %}
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="err-h">
  <h2 id="err-h" class="type-h2">Error</h2>
  <p>An error adds a red rule along the bottom, an icon, and a message in words that starts with "Error:". The field is marked <code>aria-invalid</code> and the message is linked with <code>aria-describedby</code>. The focus ring stays black, never red.</p>
  {% demo "Field with an error", "form" %}
  {{ field("ff-email", "email", "Email", type="email", autocomplete="email", error="Error: Enter a valid email address.") }}
  {% enddemo %}
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="other-h">
  <h2 id="other-h" class="type-h2">Disabled and sending</h2>
  <p>A disabled field uses the faint grey text (<code>text/neutral/disabled</code>) and a quiet rule. WCAG does not ask disabled controls to meet contrast.</p>
  {% demo "Disabled field", "form" %}
  {{ field("ff-dis", "disabled", "Disabled", disabled=true) }}
  {% enddemo %}
  <p>While a form is sending, its fields are in the sending state. They are read-only, not disabled, so their values still submit and the fields can still be reached with the keyboard. The text is still the person's own words, so it uses secondary text (<code>text/neutral/secondary</code>), which meets 4.5:1 contrast, not the disabled grey. In the macro, <code>sending=true</code> turns a field read-only. Any read-only field uses the sending look, whether or not a form is sending.</p>
  <p>Set <code>aria-busy="true"</code> on the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> while it submits, and remove it afterward, whether the send worked or failed. It tells a screen reader the form is busy. The demo below leaves it off on purpose, so this page does not stay busy. It is shown here as code only.</p>
  <pre class="demo__code" style="padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;background:var(--arche-surface-brand-primary-subtle)" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;form aria-busy="true"&gt;
  &lt;input class="field__input" readonly ...&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>
  {% demo "Sending: single-line and multi-line fields", "form" %}
  <form>
  {{ field("ff-send", "sending", "Sending", value="Typed text", sending=true) }}
  {{ field("ff-send-message", "sending-message", "Message", multiline=true, value="Typed text", sending=true) }}
  </form>
  {% enddemo %}
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="states-h">
  <h2 id="states-h" class="type-h2">States</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Field states, scrollable">
    <table class="docs-table type-small">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Bottom rule</th><th scope="col">Other</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Rest</th><td>1px, strong neutral</td><td>Label inside the box</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Hover</th><td>2px</td><td>No fill change</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Focus</th><td>2px</td><td>4px focus ring, label floats up</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Error</th><td>1px alert (2px on focus)</td><td>Icon and "Error:" message below</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Disabled</th><td>1px, quiet</td><td>Faint grey text (<code>text/neutral/disabled</code>), no hover change</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sending</th><td>1px, quiet</td><td>Secondary text (<code>text/neutral/secondary</code>), no hover change. Read-only, still sent. Set <code>aria-busy="true"</code> on the form while it submits</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Color says valid or invalid. Stroke thickness says interaction. Those are two separate channels, so neither is the only signal.</p>
</section>
